+## X2 task T1: exec-equivalent job control for `run` (2026-10-09)
+
+Task: make `run` behave like plain exec of the child in the five TASK.md situations S1-S5, on darwin-arm64 and linux-arm64, under bash and zsh, without breaking anything else. All results below were observed in this workspace; the shared harness was run read-only.
+
+### Causes found (baseline = the sources at the start of the task)
+
+1. **darwin: a SIGSTOP stop was taken for an exit.** Go's `syscall.WaitStatus` (and x/sys `unix.WaitStatus`) on BSD compares the stop signal with SIGSTOP where `<sys/wait.h>` compares it with 0x13 (SIGCONT). A child stopped by SIGSTOP therefore decoded as "continued", neither stopped nor signaled, and `ExitStatus()` returned -1: heimdall-proto exited 255, wrote `exit_code: -1` to the meta file and left the child stopped in a group that the kernel then hung up (§9.5 finding). This broke S2 (SIGSTOP), S4 and S5 on darwin.
+2. **SIGSTOP was mirrored as SIGTSTP.** The shell's `$?` was 128+SIGTSTP instead of 128+SIGSTOP (Linux 148 vs 147). This broke S2 (SIGSTOP) and S5 on Linux.
+3. **Every stop was mirrored, whoever sent it.** In modeExclusive a stop of the child always stopped heimdall-proto. With a non-interactive parent (S1) that has a controlling terminal but does not give heimdall-proto the foreground, a third party's SIGSTOP/SIGTSTP of the child stopped heimdall-proto too; the third party then continued only the child, so heimdall-proto stayed stopped forever and never reaped the child.
+
+### Change (internal/run)
+
+- `waitstatus_unix.go` (new): `wstatus`, a decoder of the raw wait status that follows the C macros on both OSes. `startAndWait` uses it for stops, exits and deaths.
+- `run_unix.go` `mirrorStop`/`shouldMirror`/`stopSelf`:
+  - A stop is mirrored only when the shell can see it: the job holds the terminal foreground (the child's group or heimdall-proto's), or the stop is SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU (terminal access from the background, which a job-control shell lists as stopped). A third party's stop while the job is not in the foreground is left alone: heimdall-proto keeps waiting and exits with the child's status (S1).
+  - The mirror uses the child's own stop signal, SIGSTOP included, so `$?` and the notice ("Stopped", "suspended (signal)") equal plain exec. SIGTTOU stays mapped to SIGTSTP (heimdall-proto ignores SIGTTOU for tcsetpgrp, and os/signal cannot restore its default).
+  - heimdall-proto stops itself with `kill(getpid(), sig)` instead of `kill(0, sig)` (K6a: never another group member).
+  - Orphaned group (no shell can see a stop): as before, TSTP/TTIN/TTOU stops are undone by continuing the child (the kernel would have discarded them for plain exec); a SIGSTOP stop is now left in place, as plain exec would be.
+  - Unchanged: after its own SIGCONT, heimdall-proto hands the terminal to the child only if its group holds the foreground, and always continues the child. That is what makes S3 work: a third party's SIGCONT to heimdall-proto resumes the child, the child's background read stops it with SIGTTIN, and that stop is mirrored as SIGTTIN.
+- S2 uses the permitted deviation (b): when a third party continues only the child while heimdall-proto is stopped, the shell keeps listing the job as stopped until `fg`, which ends it with the child's status.
+
+### Tests added
+
+- `internal/run/waitstatus_test.go`: synthetic status words; real children stopped by SIGSTOP and SIGTSTP (native decode, the darwin regression); `shouldMirror` table.
+- `cmd/heimdall-proto/jobctl_test.go`: S1-S5 end to end, each with a plain-exec control in the same environment (the control must behave as TASK.md describes, else the test fails as a precondition). S1 runs a non-shell parent (`hp-jc-s1`) without and with a controlling terminal, stdio files or pipes, SIGSTOP and SIGTSTP. S2-S5 drive `bash --norc --noprofile -i` and `zsh -f -i` on a pty. The child is the test binary (`hp-jc-child`). `-short` skips the shell scenarios.
+- Red check: the new cmd tests on the baseline `run_unix.go` (darwin) fail S1 (ctty, files, both signals), S2, S4 and S5 (proto variants); S3 passed already.
+
+### Known gaps (unchanged or introduced)
+
+- A third party's SIGSTOP/SIGTSTP of the child while the job is in the background of an interactive shell (`&` or `bg`) is not mirrored; plain exec would show "Stopped". The child is stopped while the shell lists the job as running until the third party continues it.
+- S2 (b): the job finishes only at `fg`, not on its own.
+- S3: the shell can list the job as running during a short window (not measured) between heimdall-proto's continue and the child's SIGTTIN stop being mirrored (plain exec has the same "running" window, but with the child running).
+- A SIGTTOU stop is reported as SIGTSTP.
+- An explicit SIGTSTP/SIGSTOP sent to the heimdall-proto PID still stops only heimdall-proto (§9.4, recorded; not in T1).
+- A non-shell parent that makes heimdall-proto's group the terminal foreground (a one-job shell such as the harness's pty_shim) and then has a third party stop and continue only the child would still leave heimdall-proto stopped: from inside, that is indistinguishable from a job-control shell.
